A||||17||BellaFiero||Does Race Matter?||||1||
Z||000000||BellaFiero||09-27-2000||11:15 AM||||Over the last couple of years, my girlfriends’ parents or friend’s parents, etc. , have all asked me what heritage I am. A lot of people are very interested… I have a very dark, olive skin tone with stereotypical Native American ( Dakota plains ) hair and eyebrows, long eyelashes, etc., but my last name is Friesner, decidedly German.  I clock in at 6’3” and 135 lbs, very lithe but very lean from working out. Well, anyways, these older folks always want to know what heritage I am. When I tell them that my father’s side, pure German, and my mother’s side, almost Pure Native American ( of various ‘tribes’ )… they ask “How did that happen?” …. WHAT!? HOW did that HAPPEN? Uh, gee, they… oh, I don’t know, they FELL IN LOVE! How about that one? It’s like people expect me to be  wearing Sommerkomando uniforms while riding my horses bareback with warpaint on my face!  Does race matter anymore? I mean, I know it’s important to have culture and identity, but face it – most American kids don’t have that anymore. I was raised in Oman, near Saudi in the middle east, attended a British Private Academy, and am now living in the States. But I was taught that a person is a PERSON, not a STEREOTYPE. Do you find it the same way? It’s not traditional black-and-white racism… it’s just.. I don’t know, more of a ‘German people are Bad and ‘Injuns’ can’t be trusted’ mentality I find here in the States…  I’m sure there’s more for other “races”, relgions, etc… but that’s just what I have experienced.||||reg||1
Z||000001||whamel||09-27-2000||01:33 PM||||Sadly, race and all that do seem to make a big difference to a lot of people.  Age seems to be quite a factor as well for some reason.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  I'll never understand it myself - it's not what you look like that makes you who you are - it's who you are that makes you who you are.<BR>I am constantly shocked at how some people treat other people and it amazes me the assumptions people make based on first impression.  <BR>I do have to say that I like places on the Inet (like this place) where you have NO idea who I am, where I'm from, or what I look like and mostly don't care.  It's cool that we all have one common thing to agree on here and although we have differences in opinion, the underlying premise is all the same.||||reg||1
Z||000002||SpeedPhreak||09-27-2000||02:01 PM||||i agree w/whamel.  especially in the united states.. i also think we have a problem w/being americans.. ie.. "what heritage are you?"  i mean.. if u r born in germany, speak german then u r german.. but if you are born in america, speak english, you think you are what ever nationality your great, great, great, grandparents are from.. WRONG you are AMERICAN.<P>i hate the "race" labels & color identification.. i dont think they should be tallied or recorded.. but for an example i am considered white.. i date women from dark dark dark to pale pale pale.. one of my favorite things is when im out w/a girl that is darker than me.. the looks we get are priceless.<P>just pride yourself on the fact that you are not as ignorant as a lot of people.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||09-27-2000||10:55 PM||||Bella,<BR>One thing about this country is that it is a giant melting pot. Many thousands of people come here every year. With the exception of Native Americans, <I>everybody here</I> has ancestors that came from someplace else. Many times, people are just curious. I know I am, sometimes. Not that it's any of their (or my) business. <BR>I'm usually a private person, and like to respect others' privacy, as well. I consider it an imposition to ask personal questions. Most other people are more outspoken, and will just ask, not meaning any harm. <BR>Obviously, I can't comment on the vibes you receive from these other folks, but I suspect that they don't mean to be judgemental. Only curious. <BR>Please recognize that when people are faced with a scenario that is "uncommon", they are likely to make <I>some</I> observation.<BR>(Like a friend's roommate. Mother was Hawaiian, and father was Irish. Looks *totally* Hawaiian. Name is O'Roarke. Not a big deal. Just not the red-haired pale skinned Irish guy that you'd expect when you hear the name.)<BR>I hope this helps, and doesn't offend. People don't always have a hidden agenda. They're just curious (and maybe a bit rude, sometimes.)  <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR>88 Formula T-Top <BR>88 Coupe (soon to receive transplant.)<BR>88 "pretzeled" Formula <P>Out of my mind.<BR>Back in five minutes.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DjKid||09-28-2000||12:10 AM||||Okey, If Ya'll wanna know what race I am.. hehe lots of ppl wonder.. weirdo I am.. I am human.,... I come from Earth!<P>------------------<BR>Ray - Blue 88 Coupe - <A HREF="http://blueknight.carolinafieros.com" TARGET=_blank>My Blue Knight</A> - <A HREF="http://www.carolinafieros.com" TARGET=_blank> Carolina Fieros</A>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Patrick||09-28-2000||01:08 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, lucidatypewriter">quote:</font><HR>With the exception of Native Americans, everybody here has ancestors that came from someplace else.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE> The "native" people of North and South America originally came from Asia. So technically, ALL of us have ancestors that came from someplace else.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cheever2||09-28-2000||02:14 AM||||They COULD have been just curious, like I am when I hear a foreign accent... I don't mind asking about it.  But considering the way you received it, it MUST have been ignorance.  Americans are quick to find out what's DIFFERENT about someone, rather than look for what's in common.  They fear the unknown and anything or anyone different.  So they're in a hurry to put a label on you.  I'm frankly tired of it, but I'm in the white male group, and I know the minorities are way beyond being tired of it.<p>[This message has been edited by Cheever2 (edited 09-28-2000).]||||reg||9
Z||000007||Cheever2||09-28-2000||02:19 AM||||Let me add one more thing:  I don't want us to NOT see each others' color.  We are each hopefully proud of our heritage.  I think we should CELEBRATE our differences, not fear them.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||DJRice||09-28-2000||05:57 PM||||I suggest we all start calling ourselves Fiero-Americans.  <P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"You mean like the back seat of a Volkswagen?"||||reg||1
Z||000009||Patrick||09-28-2000||10:28 PM||||<BR>Sorry Dillon, but that won't go over very well up here! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000010||hugh||09-29-2000||02:14 AM||||You say you have dark olive skin tone.Does that mean you are green?Personally,I don't care as long as you don't care that my skin is white.You have to accept people for what they are,some are curious,some are ignorant,some are just thoughtless.But I am sure that the majority are merely curious and their intentions are not to insult you.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||09-29-2000||02:37 AM||||Race doesn't matter to me, personally although it is a big deal to many, unfortunately.  I happen to be a WASP, but I have a Korean sister (adopted when she was 6 months old--she's in her late 20s now).  My great-great grandparents came over from Scotland.  They were from the Highlands and can trace their ancestry back to the days of the Vikings.  I haven't looked at my family tree recently (I couldn't care less) but I think my family was originally Norman.  I know that we used to be Vikings so maybe I'm (very) distantly related to Cliff. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  (Didn't the Vikings come from the Neatherlands?)  <BR>The town I live in is, unfortunately, very white.  There are very few people of other races/nationalities and the few that are, aren't made to feel very welcome.  Too bad because I know a few of the black folks personally and they happen to be some of the coolest I've met.  Elwood, Indiana (about 35 miles south of Wabash) is the headquarters of the largest KKK chapter in America.  Apparently, some of that BS has migrated north.<P>As long as you're comfortable with who and what you are, don't let it bother you.  Some people are and will always be morons.||||reg||1
Z||000012||My7Fieros||09-29-2000||07:16 AM||||Well it depends on what part of the country you're in also. Here, Memphis has an extrememly race-tensioned past. We dont have the "Native American-German" thing here, and we dont have the "European American-Hispanic" thing here either. Memphis is south, and old south. Race relations have always been poor here. <BR>Memphis is about 75% black...and its unfortunanlty painfully obvious. This may sound bad, but alot, if not most blacks, have a major chip on thier shoulder here.  Im sure the history of this city, as well as other large southern cities such as Birmingham, Atlanta, Charlette, ect. have alot to do with it. Its truly unfortunant the way minorties were treated in the past here. However, I feel in this day and age, its time to move on. I tend to get very upset at the double standards that are going on now...by that I mean that if you're black (or anything other than white) you can easily sue or just complain about someone "being racist" or "violating their civil rights" ...and I mean about ANYTHING. And dont think it dosnt happen every day here. However, if you're white, NO ONE will listen to you...hmmmm.<BR>Listen, I dont want anyone on here to think Im some sort of redneck racist klan member or anything like that. What I am doing is stating the facts, really. <BR>Its probobly hard for someone in another part of the country (like the Dakotas) to understand. But please try to understand that in my line of work, combined with where I live, this is the way it is. Im constanly called "white devil", "cracker", and usually "white mother f**ker"....of course they have every right to say that (sticks and stones...remember?)...now it just rolls off my back..whatever.<BR>So to rap all this mumbo jumbo up, what Im trying to say is that yes, like it or not, race does play a role today....and depending on what part of the country you're in, you will have your own race problems to deal with.||||reg||1
Z||000013||BellaFiero||09-29-2000||09:37 AM||||Wow... you guys have some awesome input... some may have been curious, but others... well. When they find out I'm BOTH German AND Native American ( god I hate that term ), it's like the worst of two worlds combined and made me. And now I'm labelled and ready to be shipped off away from their children ( lest they have BOTH Nazi and Kill the White Man influence in their lives by being my friend ). Olive skin tone and being green? Well, as my friend Kermit once said, it's aint easy... ahem. No, Olive skin tone is like an off bronze… looks like a permanent tan but not brown. Thanks for the humor.<P>It's just horrible that in this day and age of world-wide communication and connectivity that we, as a people, have to resort to using ethnicity and skin color to make their judgements on people. I'm not saying their aren't people out there we should hate, I'm saying let's base it on something other than where they're parents' parents were from.<P>Jayse <BR>-----------------<BR>Since when did Vo vohnst du mean I declare war on America??????<p>[This message has been edited by BellaFiero (edited 09-29-2000).]||||reg||12
Z||000014||Cheever2||10-01-2000||04:27 AM||||Saw Remember The Titans on Friday.  Great movie.  The whole family liked it.  Everybody in the theater liked it.  Very well done.  Clean language too.  Laughs, tears, and hard-hitting football (American style).  What else could you ask for?  Don't answer that.  Go see it.<P>||||reg||14
Z||000015||Songman||10-04-2000||12:09 AM||||I'm from the South too and have found that things that My7Fieros said to be all too true. I have never been one that has been bothered by any aspect of another human being other than their character. That is the measure of a person. I have very close friends of all races, colors, religions, etc...and it don't bother any of us.<P>But, it is true that reverse racism is a major thing and I believe it is not only in the South. I used to joke that I would like to enter myself in the Miss Black America contest and see how much people yelled since I am white and male. Caucasians as a race would be crucified if we tried to have Miss White America or White Entertainment Television. If you want equality, I am all for it.. if you want payback you are in the wrong game. I had nothing to do with slavery.<P>One more thing... well, actually two since we are on the subject... What's up with this African-American thing? If you were born in Africa and moved to America.. fine. But that is not the case mostly. Should I be German, Dutch, Scottish American just because my ancestors came from somewhere else. If that is the case the only true Americans are Native American Indians... oh yeah! I have Cherokee blood too! Why can't we just all be Americans when we live in this country?<P>Second part of this - What's up with the rage against the Confederate flag? It is not a sign of slavery. If you still believe that the Civil War was about slavery you need to do some more studying. That was just a nice political agenda. I love being from the South and I love our history. I don't agree with everything that happened here but it is still my heritage. They want their heritage intact and so do I.<P>Okay... somebody knock me down off this soapbox! heheh. ||||reg||7
Z||000016||ur2biteme||10-12-2000||09:05 AM||||i used to live in the south, throuought Alabama and Gerogia and encountered the before mentioned reverse-racism.  in fact, when i tell people about the city i used to live in (LaGrange GA)  i ask them if they remember the b.s. back in '94 about the intteracial couple that wanted to attend their prom and weren't allowed to by the principal.  that was in Roanoake AL.  8 miles from my former home.  i felt real great about the fact that those untollerant morons of the KKK were marching the streets of a town 8 miles from the one i lived in.  makes my stomach turn.  <P>i'd like to know what they'd have thought about my ex girlfriend.  half hispanic.  cant stand her now, but that's because she's a, well i'll spare the details.  but it's personal reasons, not racial.  <P>the thing i've found about CA to be different as far as the racial thing is that there really doesn't seem to be that much racial hating out here.  everyone hates eachother equally.  i'm not cynical.  not in the least   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>oh.  i guess i share a similar background.  i'm about 3/4 english & german, 1/4 native american.  i usually say that i'd be repressing myself.  i'm not dark though, but i have the trim native american features and i always look like i have a very slight tan, but i never get darker in the sun.  i immediately scorch.  oh well....<BR><P>------------------<BR>UR2BITEME<BR>'87 econo coupe<BR>170k and a bad fender :(||||reg||1
Z||000017||2xsess||10-14-2000||06:32 PM||||Ok, here's my bit,I'm from northeastern PA, my grandfather once peat my mother for talking to the black boy next door. Now imagine this, my wife is from Jamaica, yes she's black so we have exprienced both racism and the reverse racism that has been mentioned here. I agree with the comment made about liking a person for their personality rather than their race.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT||||reg||1
